# -*- mode:org; coding:utf-8 -*-
#+TITLE: ccl-protocol-write-atomic-file-contention-hangs — PROTOCOL-WRITE-ATOMIC-FILE-CONTENTION never returns under CCL on the macOS dev box
#+STATUS: TODO
#+PRIORITY: P3
#+TYPE: bug
#+DEPENDS: -
#+BRANCH: fix-ccl-protocol-write-atomic-file-contention-hangs

* Symptom

2026-09-21, macOS arm64, CCL =/usr/local/src/ccl/dx86cl64= (x86-64, so under
Rosetta): =make -C autolisp-front-end test-ccl= stops making progress at

  Running test PROTOCOL-WRITE-ATOMIC-FILE-CONTENTION

with the process idle (0 % CPU) for over ten minutes. The test alone,
=(fiveam:run! 'protocol-write-atomic-file-contention)= in a fresh CCL, is
killed by a 120 s =timeout=. The same test passes under SBCL (part of the
suite's green run).

* What is known / not known

- Found while running the alfe suite for the plug-in system
  (=alfe-plugin-model=). The plug-in work does not touch it
  (=write-atomic-file= and its threads are unchanged; only
  =emit-run-common-lsp= changed in that file).
- NOT verified at the parent commit: the tree was not stashed (there are
  unrelated stashes on this box), so "pre-existing" is the strong suspicion,
  not a measurement.
- Related closed issue: =write-atomic-file-concurrent-rename-windows=.

* Proposal

Run the test at =HEAD~= on the same box to settle "pre-existing"; then find
out whether it is Rosetta / the x86 CCL (an arm64 CCL is a separate issue,
=cecil-native-arm64-runner=) or a real deadlock in the contention path
(=bordeaux-threads= under CCL). Until then =make test-ccl= cannot be used to
completion on this machine; run subsets by test name.

* Measured 2026-09-26 — not CCL, and not this code: the same test passes in seconds on Linux

Ran the test ALONE under =CCL Version 1.13 (v1.13-22-g3c7de4fd) LinuxX8664= —
the same CCL release as the macOS box's, x86-64, but NATIVE rather than under
Rosetta:

: Running test PROTOCOL-WRITE-ATOMIC-FILE-CONTENTION ...
:  Did 3 checks.  Pass: 3 (100%)  Fail: 0 (0%)

Seconds, not ten minutes. Five repetitions, all green, and the full
=make -C autolisp-front-end test-ccl= completes (1228 checks, 0 failures).

So two candidate causes are OUT: a deadlock in =write-atomic-file='s contention
path, and =bordeaux-threads= under CCL as such. What is left is the macOS box
specifically — =dx86cl64= under *Rosetta* (16 writer threads + a reader, x86
threading emulated on arm64), or something about that machine. That is where
the next measurement has to be taken, and =cecil-native-arm64-runner= /a native
arm64 CCL/ is the obvious lever.

* A stall now fails instead of hanging (clautolisp 2.2.112)

The worst part of this was never the hang, it was the SILENCE: ten minutes at
0 % CPU, no output, nothing said about which thread was stuck, and
=make test-ccl= unusable to completion on that machine. Fixed regardless of the
root cause:

- =%JOIN-THREADS-OR-NAME-THE-STUCK= replaces the bare =JOIN-THREAD= loop
  (bordeaux-threads 0.9 has no portable join-with-timeout, so it polls
  =THREAD-ALIVE-P=). After =+CONTENTION-DEADLINE+= (120 s, against ~2 s for a
  healthy run) it destroys the stragglers and returns their NAMES, which the
  test turns into a failure: /Threads still running after 120 s:
  ("atomic-writer-7" …)/ — precisely the evidence the box could not produce.
- It then WAITS for the destroyed threads to actually die (=DESTROY-THREAD=
  asks, it does not wait), so a wedged thread is not inherited by the next
  test. Found by measurement: the watchdog's own test failed on that race the
  first time it ran.
- =protocol-contention-watchdog-names-a-thread-that-will-not-finish= proves the
  watchdog FIRES — it rebinds the deadline to 1 s against a thread that will
  not finish and asserts the name comes back and the thread is gone. A watchdog
  never seen doing its job is worth nothing.

Suites: alfe 1228 checks, 0 failures, on SBCL *and* CCL; the watchdog test
green five runs in a row under CCL.

* What remains

- [ ] The macOS/Rosetta measurement: run =make -C autolisp-front-end test-ccl=
      on that box. It should now either pass or FAIL WITHIN ~2 MINUTES naming
      the stuck threads. Either outcome moves this forward; the previous one
      did not.
- [ ] =HEAD~= comparison there is no longer worth its cost: the Linux result
      above says the contention code is not the cause, so "pre-existing" is
      answered for practical purposes.
